.TH TUXDISPLAY 1 "September 2026" "TuxDisplay 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tuxdisplay \- use an iPad browser as a secondary Linux monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tuxdisplay
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B TuxDisplay
creates a compositor-owned virtual monitor in a GNOME Wayland session and publishes it to an authenticated browser viewer. Windows can be dragged between the physical display and the iPad. A GTK manager and tray indicator show disconnected, running, and connected states. Other desktops use an isolated X11/noVNC workspace as a fallback.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start, stop, restart
Control the per-user display service.
.TP
.B status
Show service state, display mode, browser URL, and access PIN.
.TP
.B url
Print the preferred iPad URL.
.TP
.B password [--reset]
Show or regenerate the six-digit access PIN.
.TP
.B launch APPLICATION [ARGUMENT ...]
Start an application in the current desktop. In X11 fallback mode, start it in the isolated workspace.
.TP
.B usb connect|disconnect|status
Detect and manage iPad USB tethering or a supported USB gadget connection.
.TP
.B tray
Run the GNOME tray indicator with start, USB connect, and close-connection actions.
.TP
.B doctor
Check display mode, dependencies, networking, and USB gadget capability.
.TP
.B gui
Open the standalone GTK manager. This is the default when no command is given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/config
Resolution and port settings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/password
Generated access PIN. The file is readable only by its user.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tuxdisplay/
Service state and application logs.
.SH SECURITY
The web listener is available on all interfaces and uses PIN authentication. Traffic is ordinary HTTP. Use it only on a trusted USB or private LAN link and do not expose port 6080 to the public Internet.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tuxdisplay-usb (8)
